 shows how these household characteristics correlate with the decision to restrict supply. Renters and households that expect to buy additional housing tend to support additional permits because new supply lowers purchase and rental costs. These households tend to be younger, have lower incomes and hold less wealth. By contrast, homeowners who are unlikely to buy additional housing tend to support restrictions because lower supply preserves the resale value of their housing wealth and the size of their bequest.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The induced equilibrium price of housing therefore varies with the relative size of these groups. An exogenous increase in young households, who disproportionately rent and expect to buy housing in the future, increases political support for additional supply. The house price then falls until the median household is indifferent over a marginal change in housing permits.
\end_layout

