\begin_layout Section
Numerical Algorithm
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
rev{
\end_layout

\end_inset

The numerical solution has two parts. The first solves the stationary political-economic accounting equilibrium for a fixed demographic structure. The second solves transition paths under a specified rule for expectations.
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Stationary Equilibrium
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For a fixed demographic structure, the numerical solution follows these steps. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The household's value functions are solved recursively for a fixed value of 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{h}$
\end_inset

, starting in the final period of life and working backwards. This provides solutions for the household value functions 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ V^{rent}_{j},V^{adjust}_{j},V^{non-adjust}_{j}\right\} $
\end_inset

 and the resulting decision rules for consumption, housing and liquid assets as a function of the households' idiosyncratic state variables. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The rental price of housing is pinned down by the cost of housing 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{h}$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
We then calculate how these value functions change if housing supply is marginally restricted. Operationally, this is evaluated through the small persistent price change 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon^{p}$
\end_inset

 implied by lower supply. The difference in value functions is used to calculate preferences over housing supply across households, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Next we solve for the density of households across the life cycle using these decision rules to solve for the distribution of households across the idiosyncratic state variables. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
rev{
\end_layout

\end_inset

We then use these solutions to calculate the share of households voting to restrict housing supply, 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p^{h})$
\end_inset

. We solve for the root of 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p^{h})-0.5=0$
\end_inset

 using a bracketed price search. This avoids relying on a directional update rule and directly implements the median-voter condition.
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
rev{
\end_layout

\end_inset

Given fixed age weights and household formation rates, we iterate these decision rules until the cross-sectional distribution is stationary under the imposed demographic structure.
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Transition Paths with Forecast Rules
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

